  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a simulation model for a single country, and the projected emission reductions depend on the assumption that the ban is implemented early and comprehensively.",
      "what": "The study introduces I3E-Transport, a passenger transport model linked to a computable general equilibrium framework, to simulate Irish transport emissions, vehicle stock, and energy demand from 2024 to 2050. The simulations reveal that while a carbon tax achieves minimal emission reductions, a complete ban on internal combustion engine vehicles (including hybrids) starting in 2035 cuts CO2 emissions by over five million tonnes relative to the baseline by 2050.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that standard carbon taxation is insufficient for deep decarbonization in passenger transit, proving that only an immediate and comprehensive ban on fossil-fuel vehicles can align the sector with national carbon budget targets."
    }
